<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>4.12.6: Practice</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01081</md:content-id>
    <md:title>4.12.6: Practice</md:title>
    <md:uuid>42f852cd-267d-4c26-b848-c79509100376</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><para>Complete the following questions to practice the skills you have learned in this lesson.</para>
<section class="check-understanding"><exercise id="445b191e-c8fd-46a7-b4ba-3d8d1acdf66f" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>
          The cost for an upcoming field trip is $15 per student. The cost of the field trip \(C\), in dollars, is a function of the number of students \(x\).
          <emphasis>Select two</emphasis> possible outputs for the function defined by \(C(x)=15x\).
        </para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! 30, 90</para><para>Try again. Revisit activities 4.12.1-4.12.2. </para><para>The correct answers are 30, 90. </para></solution></exercise><exercise id="e3786363-f2c0-4c5e-9873-59843229d9d0" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>A rectangle has an area of \(24 cm^2\). Function \(f\) gives the length of the rectangle, in centimeters, when the width is \(w\) cm.
          Determine which values, in centimeters, are possible inputs of the function.
          <emphasis>Select three</emphasis> that apply.
        </para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! 3, 0.5, and 48 are possible inputs. Not possible: -6, 0.</para><para>Not quite right. Consider the factors of 24 to find possible widths.</para><para>The correct answers are 3, 0.5, and 48 are possible inputs. Not possible: -6, 0.</para></solution></exercise><exercise id="3e4feb7c-ba40-4446-a165-e68e84084e4b" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para><emphasis>Select three</emphasis> possible input-output pairs for the function \(y=x^3\).</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct!  \((-1,-1)\), \(\left(\frac12,\frac18\right)\), \((4,64)\)</para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 4.12.3. </para><para>The correct answers are: \((1,-1)\), \(\left(\frac12,\frac18\right)\), \((4,64)\). </para></solution></exercise><exercise id="93103872-a3b8-4df7-9763-85f8e25df230" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>Two functions are defined by the equations \(f(x)=5−0.2x\) and \(g(x)=0.2(x+5)\). 
            
          <emphasis>Select two</emphasis> possible statements that are true about the functions.
        </para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! </para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 4.12.3</para><para>The correct answers are \(f(3)&gt;0\), \(g(-1)=0.8\). </para></solution></exercise><exercise id="47195a33-5c1b-46a6-8283-458cc16086e9" class="multiple-choice"><problem><newline/><para>Is the relation graphed below a function?</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! No, this is not a function because 4 maps to both -1 and −3.</para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 4.12.3. </para><para>The correct answer is; No, this is not a function because 4 maps to both -1 and −3. </para></solution></exercise><exercise id="77029a4d-3998-48ee-adfb-b3ccd2b2c34e" class="multiple-choice"><problem><newline/><para>Is the relation graphed below a function?</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! no; This is not a function because −3 maps to both 4 and −4. </para><para>Try again. Revisit activities 4.12.3.</para><para>The correct answer is no; This is not a function because −3 maps to both 4 and −4. </para></solution></exercise><exercise id="783c6eec-081f-41b1-9e43-078fa58bfd52" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>Use the following scenario for questions 7 - 9.</para><para><quote>For a fundraiser, Chi and her class are having a talent show and charging $7 a ticket. There are 100 seats in the theater. The function \(T\), gives the total for selling \(n\) tickets and is represented by \(T(n)=7n\).</quote></para><para>Enter the minimum domain value.
        </para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! The minimum domain value is 0</para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 4.12.5. </para><para>The correct answer is that the minimum domain value is 0. </para></solution></exercise><exercise id="707cc6fb-1ff4-4f84-b98b-3ea6f7155506" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>Enter the maximum domain value.

        </para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! The maximum domain value is 100.</para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 4.12.5. </para><para>The correct answer is that the maximum domain value is 100. </para></solution></exercise><exercise id="de1ba9ff-c4e5-45b0-a9b9-c2d4d207c08b" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para> Is 30 a possible output in the range?</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! No; 30 is not a possible value in the range because all values of the range must be multiples of 7. </para><para>Try again. Revisit activities 4.12.5.</para><para>The correct answer is 30 is not a possible value in the range because all values of the range must be multiples of 7. </para></solution></exercise></section></section>
</content>
